A GPU node is basically a server that contains several GPUs. 
In this example, we have two GPU nodes, and each node contains eight NVIDIA A100 GPUs. So, in total, we have 16 GPUs.

The GPUs inside the same node need to communicate with each other very quickly. 
This is where NVIDIA NVLink comes in. 
NVLink is a high-speed interconnect that allows GPUs inside a node to exchange data efficiently.

NVLink = high-speed GPU interconnect inside the server/node.
Network Fabric = high-speed network connecting GPU nodes.

Now, we have two separate GPU nodes, so we also need a way to connect the nodes together. This is the role of the network fabric.

You can think of the network fabric as a high-speed communication system connecting the GPU nodes. In this example, 
each GPU node connects to the network fabric with 1.6 terabits per second, which is 1,600 gigabits per second.

Since each node contains eight GPUs, the course explains the bandwidth proportionally:

1,600 Gbps ÷ 8 GPUs = 200 Gbps per GPU.

So the idea is that each GPU has approximately 200 Gbps of proportional bandwidth in this simplified example.

The network fabric is also described as nonblocking. This means that the network is designed with enough capacity so that multiple GPUs can communicate
at the same time without the network fabric becoming a bottleneck under the intended traffic pattern.

This is especially important for large AI and deep learning workloads. A large AI model may use many GPUs at the same time. 
These GPUs need to exchange data, parameters, and intermediate results. If the communication between GPUs is slow, 
the GPUs may spend time waiting for data instead of performing computations.

**So the overall picture is:

GPU node → contains 8 GPUs
NVLink → connects GPUs inside the node
Network fabric → connects GPU nodes
1.6 Tbps → bandwidth from each node to the fabric
Nonblocking → allows concurrent GPU communication without the fabric becoming the bottleneck

And this is the infrastructure that allows large AI workloads, such as training and running large models, to use many GPUs together efficiently.

**************Supercluster**************
A Supercluster is a much larger GPU environment than a normal cluster. It can contain tens of thousands of GPUs, 
and the architecture can potentially scale beyond 100,000 GPUs.

Because the number of GPUs is so large, the Supercluster is divided into blocks. Each block contains a group of GPUs and its networking infrastructure.

To connect these large numbers of GPUs efficiently, the system uses a three-tier CLOS network. 
CLOS is a scalable network design that uses multiple layers of switches instead of requiring every GPU to have a direct connection to every other GPU.

GPU A ──┐
GPU B ──┼── Switch ── Switch ── GPU X
GPU C ──┘                   └── GPU Y


The benefit is scalability. However, there is a trade-off: 
when a GPU in Block 1 communicates with a GPU in a distant Block N, 
the traffic may pass through more network components and travel a longer physical distance. Therefore, the latency is higher.

Inside one block, the round-trip latency in the example is about 6.5 microseconds. 
Across the large Supercluster, the worst-case round-trip latency can be around 20 microseconds.

20 microseconds is still very small, but the higher latency needs to be considered when designing the network. 
This is why the network uses appropriate buffering and QoS (Quality of Service) mechanisms.

QoS = managing network traffic to maintain the required service/performance.

Buffers temporarily hold packets when traffic is waiting to move through the network. 
The switches and networking chips are selected with enough buffering, and the buffers are properly allocated for the GPU workloads.

This becomes especially important because the Supercluster is designed to support RDMA, or Remote Direct Memory Access. 
RDMA is used for high-performance communication between systems, which is very important for large AI workloads where GPUs exchange large amounts of data.

The key concept is lossless networking. In a lossless network, the switches are designed to avoid dropping packets because of congestion. 
The network uses sufficient buffering and intelligent congestion-notification mechanisms to control traffic and prevent packet loss.

So the complete idea is:

