*********Control Plane*********
OCI uses a control plane to decide where a customer's workload should run. This decision is called placement.

If a workload is small enough to fit inside one block, the control plane can place the entire workload in a single block. 
This keeps the GPUs or compute nodes closer together and provides lower latency.

If the workload is much larger and requires more capacity than one block can provide, it can be placed across multiple blocks. 
This provides the required scale, but communication may have higher latency.

So the key idea is:

Small workload → Single Block → Lower latency
Large workload → Multiple Blocks → Greater scale

And:

Control Plane → decides the placement

The overall goal is to balance scale and latency through intelligent workload placement.

*********Network locality*********
Network locality means understanding which GPUs or hosts are close to each other in the network.

In this example, there are eight hosts connected through four Top-of-Rack (ToR) switches across two different blocks. 
If the workload does not know anything about the network topology, any GPU could communicate with any other GPU. 
Some of these communications might travel across longer network paths, which increases latency.

OCI makes this network locality information available to customers. 
The customer can use this information as placement hints when designing the GPU or ML topology.

For example, the customer can organize the workload so that some GPU communication stays on the same ToR switch. 
In the example, about half of the traffic stays local to the ToR switch. 
Around 85% of the traffic stays within the same block, and only a smaller portion crosses between blocks.

This is important because communication within the same ToR can have extremely low latency. 
The example says that about half of the traffic can have latency below 6.5 microseconds, 
while only some traffic needs to use the longer paths with around 20-microsecond worst-case latency.

Network locality also provides another benefit: higher throughput.

A flow is a stream of network traffic between two endpoints. 
A flow collision happens when multiple flows need to use the same network link at the same time, creating contention.

If most traffic stays local, fewer flows need to travel through shared links in the higher levels of the network. 
Therefore, the probability of flow collisions is reduced.

So network locality provides two major benefits:

Locality → shorter network paths → lower latency
and
Locality → fewer shared-link collisions → higher throughput

The main idea is that OCI does not only provide a huge GPU Supercluster. 
It also exposes information about the network topology so customers can design their workloads to communicate locally whenever possible.

